  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 258 AM CDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nCurrent Conditions/Today:\n\nLow stratus is spreading across NW WI and south of the the Twin \nPorts this morning and could leak its way west towards Aitkin. Under \nthis cloud deck there have been reports of fog and a dense fog \nadvisory may be needed later. Expect fog and cloud cover to erode \nthrough the latter morning hours giving way to another warm day with \nhighs in the 60s and 70s. The exception to this will be areas along \nthe Lake Superior where onshore flow will keep the temps closer to \nthe mid 50s to low 60s. Another round of fog will be possible tonight.\n\nFriday:\n\nA warm front will be lifting across the region Friday afternoon and \nevening. Isentropic lift associated with the boundary will lead to \nsome scattered showers populating across our western counties. \nOverall, light rain expected with the boundary passage with a \nrumble of thunder possible. \n\nSaturday:\n\nOn Saturday, a mature low pressure will be sliding across \nManitoba. It's associated cold front will be cruising across the\nNorthland providing another dose of light rain across the \nregion. MUCAPE does increase a bit along the boundary as upper \nlevels cool and mid level lapse rates increase to 7C/km. This \ncould lead to some isolated storms at times but not expecting \nanything severe. Rain chances will quickly taper off west to \neast through Saturday night as the boundary moves into Central \nWI. Additionally, out ahead of this boundary through the day the\nstrong southerly flow will lead to widespread 70s, 15-20F above\nnormal for this time of year.\n\nSunday into early next week: \n\nA return to more seasonable temperatures as surface high pressure \nsettles in behind the exiting cold front. Highs will be in the 40s \nand 50s with overnight lows dropping to below freezing. Heading into \nthe work week could see another uptick in activity but confidence \nremains low at this juncture. The 00Z suite of guidance would \nsuggest that a pair of upper level lows could impact the region as \nearly as Sunday night. The first low of concern is ejecting out of \nthe Central Rockies but will have to fight through the dry and cool \nair mass over the Northland. The overall trend has been shifting \nthis system further south over the past 4 runs with only the \nBrainerd Lakes over into NW WI now seeing a 20-40% chance of rain \ninto Monday morning. Better chances may arrive at the mid week point \nwhen an upper level trough bring better synoptic forcing. However, \nlocation and timing remain highly contested amongst current suite of \nguidance.",
